  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., has a higher probability mass on unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones). To assess skewness, we compare the distribution of outcomes. Lottery A has two outcomes: +3000 (50%) and -3000 (50%). This is symmetric around zero, so it is not skewed (skewness = 0). Lottery B has a single outcome: +2000 (100%), which is a degenerate distribution with no variation and thus no skewness in the usual sense, but it cannot be considered right-skewed because there is no probability on unfavorable outcomes relative to the single certain outcome. Since both lotteries are either symmetric or non-variable, neither is clearly more right-skewed. However, if we interpret right-skew in terms of probability weight on lower (unfavorable) outcomes relative to higher ones, Lottery A assigns 50% to a negative outcome (-3000), while Lottery B has no negative outcome. Thus, Lottery A could be seen as more left-skewed (favorable tail) or less right-skewed than B. But crucially, the reason prefers the *more right-skewed* lottery, which would be the one with more weight on bad outcomes. In this case, Lottery A has a 50% chance of a large loss, so it might be interpreted as more negatively skewed (left-skewed), not right-skewed. Therefore, the reason would likely prefer Lottery B, as it avoids the downside risk present in A. However, the concept of 'right-skew' as defined (more probability on unfavorable outcomes) would make Lottery A more right-skewed only if -3000 is considered unfavorable and +3000 favorable — which it is — so 50% probability on the unfavorable outcome. Lottery B has 100% on a medium-positive outcome. So relative to expected value or utility, A has higher spread and downside. But under the given definition: 'right-skewed if greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable as opposed to favorable outcomes', we must define what is 'unfavorable' vs 'favorable'. If we take the outcomes: in A, one favorable (+3000), one unfavorable (-3000), each 50%. In B, the outcome (2000) is less than 3000 but more than -3000. So 2000 is better than the unfavorable outcome of A but worse than the favorable. But B is certain. So is 2000 'favorable' or 'unfavorable'? Relative to A's range, it's intermediate. But since B has no unfavorable outcome (no loss), it cannot have probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. Thus, Lottery A has 50% on an unfavorable outcome, B has 0%. Therefore, A is more right-skewed under this definition. So the reason would prefer A — the more right-skewed lottery. This is counterintuitive because right-skew usually means long tail on the right (favorable), but here the definition is inverted: 'right-skewed if greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes'. That seems like a misnomer — this is more like 'negatively skewed' or 'loss-heavy'. But per the definition, the reason prefers the lottery with more probability on unfavorable outcomes — i.e., more downside risk. So A has 50% on -3000 (unfavorable), B has 0%. So A is more right-skewed (per this definition), and the reason prefers A.",
